00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.130
So first, a little
bit of background.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.440
So what's the difference between
a geometry and a geography?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.860
So when my kids draw lines
on the map, they say, oh,

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.830
how do we get from
Seattle to LA?

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.144
They just draw a line
on the map, right?

00:03:18.144 --> 00:03:20.310
They're doing Euclid--
although they don't know it--

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.140
the shortest distance between
two points is a straight line.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.200
That is perfectly accurate,
except if the surface

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.820
that you're talking
about is actually

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:32.550
curved, such as
our planet, here.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.640
So in this case, the
shortest distance

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.380
between Seattle and Stuttgart
is this great circle route.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.719
However, Euclid was not wrong.

00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:43.260
If you look at that
from space, you'd

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.720
get that straight line again.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.250
So the point of this is that if
you want to express geometries

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.290
on the surface of the
Earth, obviously you've

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:54.330
got to take the curvature
of the Earth into account,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.300
otherwise you get
the math wrong.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.860
That's really the
whole point of this.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:05.890
So can you properly do
GIS in BigQuery using SQL?

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.140
Any guesses as to what I'm--

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:08.710
OK, right.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:09.220
Thank you.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.720
I saw a couple
chuckles in the back.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.150
So the answer is yes,
and that's the capability

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.710
that we're launching right now.

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:18.850
So we are bringing geospatial
data types and geospatial

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.070
functions into BigQuery
as first class citizens.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:24.100
And that is--

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.980
I mean, I know there's
no fireworks going on,

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.500
but that is super cool.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.169
Let's press in a little bit.

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:34.710
So this is a 200
level talk, so we're

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.450
going to get into some details.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.080
I'm not going to
read all of this,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.300
but what we're launching today
is native support for many

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.120
categories of ST_* functions.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.810
So those of you who use
PostGIS will find this very,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.320
very comfortable-- and I
put this slide up here both

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.760
as reference for later viewing
and as an indication that there

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.150
really is quite a wide
swath of functionality that

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.530
we're introducing here--

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.170
from predicates to parsers
and formatters, et cetera.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.081
Here's a list-- oh, sorry.

00:05:07.081 --> 00:05:09.330
Here's a list of the data
types that we're supporting,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.780
so points, line
strings, polygons,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.420
polygons with voids in
them, multipolygons.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:19.690
And then a collection
is one or any number,

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.910
or any-- well, a collection
is one, any number,

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.410
or any combination of
all of these above.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.360
The formats we're
supporting right now

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.290
are well-known text and GeoJSON.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.310
We'll be adding support for
ingesting shape files natively

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:32.880
really soon.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.450
Here's a list of
some of the functions

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.720
that we're launching right now.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.920
A couple of our favorites--

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.760
we've got links to queries.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.090
So if you've got access
to the slides later on,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.130
you can just come
and click on this,

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.870
and it'll take you
to a BigQuery query

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.660
where you can play with the
joined based on STD within.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.390
But for those of you who
use these kinds of functions

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:59.110
or have used these kinds
of functions in the past,

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.390
this should look pretty
familiar and feel

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:04.180
pretty familiar to you.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.670
Anyway, we're super
stoked about that.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.480
But that's really only
half of the problem, right?

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.580
When you write a
query and you print

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.160
out a geography in the
table viewer portion

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:16.955
of the BigQuery UI--

00:06:16.955 --> 00:06:19.060
like for example,
there are census tracks

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.900
and there are zip codes that
have thousands of vertices.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.540
And so if you just
print out that row,

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:30.460
you get 1,000 lat/long pairs
that makes for a very poor user

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:31.510
experience, right?

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.810
Tables is not how human
beings visualize or interact

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.320
with geospatial data--

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.320
and so we have a couple
different options

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.240
for folks to do viz on
geo data inside of GCP.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.390
Number one is for
hardcore mappers.

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.200
You can go ahead and
use Earth Engine.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.280
You can export geo
data from BigQuery,

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.350
import it into Earth
Engine, and then use

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.450
the JavaScript console--

00:06:55.450 --> 00:07:00.520
which is that middle section
of tiny text right there--

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.040
to create whatever map
you want based on the data

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.870
that you just brought
in from BigQuery.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.060
So for hardcore mappers like--

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.510
so first off, it's
a bit of a pest

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.370
to have to do the
export and import,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.900
but for hardcore
mappers, they might

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.180
find that a perfectly
reasonable workflow.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.710
And in our alpha testing,
it's borne out that way.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:26.230
However, how many folks here,
they just go ahead and use SQL?

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:27.880
Quick show of hands?

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:28.380
All right.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.220
So for you all, we
have this use case.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.950
Hi, I'm an analyst.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:35.950
I like BigQuery, I
just wrote a query that

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.590
has a geospatial data
type in the result.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:42.260
Would you please let
me view that on a map?

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.850
And so to do that, we're
actually launching to Alpha

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.150
as well, BigQuery Geo Viz,
which is a lightweight mapping

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:53.980
tool that you can use to go
ahead and compose a query here

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.960
in the window--

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.880
and then you run that
query, and then it

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:02.080
calls BigQuery to run the query,
populates the results here.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.870
You tell it which result--
which column you want to draw

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.820
and it drops it onto
the map, and then you

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.680
can do real time styling.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.930
Now, this downloads
those results

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.345
all into the
browser's memory, so--

00:08:13.345 --> 00:08:14.710
oh, sorry.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:19.030
We don't need to watch
the gif go around again.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.230
It downloads the results
into the browser's memory,

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.850
so it's not an infinite cache
of geo data that you can draw.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.550
It's limited to about
2,000 points or polygons.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.260
However, for the
case where you just

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.450
want to see the
result on a map, we

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:34.150
thought this was a much
better experience than,

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.280
well, honestly anything
else we could think of.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.370
And I would like to point out
that the engineer, Mr. Don

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.950
McCurdy who did this, is sitting
right here in the front row,

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.518
so thank you very much, Don.

