Thanks, everyone.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.360
So before we get
to the demo, I just

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.360
want to explain a little
bit about who Geotab is

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:02.560
and what we do.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:05.342
So Geotab is a global
leader in a telematic space.

00:12:05.342 --> 00:12:07.300
We have an open platform
from fleet management.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.591
So for those of you that
don't know what telematics is,

00:12:09.591 --> 00:12:11.166
it's connecting
commercial vehicles

00:12:11.166 --> 00:12:13.790
and reporting where they are and
other information about them--

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:14.980
so it's kind of a
nice coincidence

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.271
that we're surrounded by all
these cool, colorful tires

00:12:17.271 --> 00:12:18.640
here.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.370
And so we have 1.2 million
vehicles-- or over 1.2 million

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.160
vehicles-- driving around
the world every single day,

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:27.190
and that produces 3 billion
rows of data each and every day

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.737
that we ingest into BigQuery.

00:12:28.737 --> 00:12:30.820
And an interesting thing
that we do a Geotab is we

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.830
have a patented data
reduction algorithm,

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:34.304
a curved-based
logging algorithm,

00:12:34.304 --> 00:12:36.220
that allows us to minimize
the amount of data.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.920
So those three billion rows,
once they land in BigQuery,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.857
we can explode those out by
interpolating into really,

00:12:41.857 --> 00:12:43.190
really large-- usually tenfold--

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:43.990
data sets.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:45.520
And I was going to come up
and explain a little bit

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:47.500
about the size of the
data we have in BigQuery,

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.249
but I just found out
from Chad before this

00:12:49.249 --> 00:12:51.790
that the stats many of you
saw this morning, the largest

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:53.290
query ever written
in terms of rows,

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.840
was one of our employees--
so 28 trillion rows, which

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:57.860
was awesome.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:58.690
So that's cool.

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:01.150
But we also believe we
have the richest data set

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:02.440
in the telematics business.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:05.200
And why do I say that is
because we're much, much more

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:06.700
than just points on a map--

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.130
although, obviously, we have
no lack of points on a map.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:12.220
This is one day of Geotab
data in North America,

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.680
but we collect a whole
host of other information

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:16.180
that's useful for
fleet management,

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:19.750
but also extremely useful
for smart city applications.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.760
So we manufacture the
device and the software,

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.020
and on device, we
have an accelerometer.

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.860
The accelerometer measures
3-axis accelerometer movements

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:29.895
in forward and back so we can
detect harsh braking incidence

00:13:29.895 --> 00:13:31.270
or hazardous
incidents that would

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.394
result from hard acceleration.

00:13:33.394 --> 00:13:34.810
We can have
side-to-side movements

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.976
so we can tell if people
are swerving around objects

00:13:36.976 --> 00:13:39.316
or if there's corners that
people are taking too fast,

00:13:39.316 --> 00:13:40.690
that maybe they're
blind corners,

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:41.980
or you need add a sign.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.140
And up/down allows us to
monitor road conditions

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.990
so we can do things like
identify potholes or bad road

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.184
conditions.

00:13:48.184 --> 00:13:49.600
Another interesting
thing we do is

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.182
we actually reverse engineer
data from the vehicle itself,

00:13:53.182 --> 00:13:55.390
and that includes a lot of
things that you'd expect--

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.450
fault codes, seat belt
usage, engine RPM--

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:00.490
but it includes some
really, really cool things

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.160
that you wouldn't think of,
like ambient air temperature

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.540
and outside pressure.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:06.250
And we can use those
things to generate

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.340
hyperlocal roadside
weather and pressure maps--

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.290
and we also get windshield
wiper information.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.799
And with 1.2 million
vehicles driving around,

00:14:13.799 --> 00:14:16.090
we can actually predict if
it's snowing or raining just

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:17.950
by the activity
of the windshield

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.450
wipers in various areas.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:21.460
And then we can combine
all of these things that

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.830
generate driver
behavior profiles

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:26.492
and tell if there's areas
in a city that are hazardous

00:14:26.492 --> 00:14:28.700
or that need some attention
because there's accidents

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:29.470
there.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:31.540
And if you want to learn
some more about what

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:35.800
we do at Geotab, we actually
just published a joint case

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:36.747
study with Google.

00:14:36.747 --> 00:14:38.080
It's a really, really good read.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.996
I'll leave it there for
the folks on YouTube--

00:14:39.996 --> 00:14:44.159
and check it out, learn a
little bit more about Geotab.

00:14:44.159 --> 00:14:46.450
Now, when Chad asked us to
talk about some of the stuff

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:48.970
we did with GIS, I wanted
to mention and make

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.600
sure we talked about this,
that Geotab has been doing

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.200
GIS in BigQuery for some time--

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.120
so we've been a longtime
BigQuery customer,

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.210
and as you can imagine,
most of our data

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.320
has a geospatial component.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.770
So over the years, we've
been developing ways

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.620
to do geospatial
analysis in BigQuery.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:08.570
And one of the coolest features
a BigQuery, in my opinion,

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:11.000
is the JavaScript
DDF functionality.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.500
So we are able to use the
JavaScript functionality to bin

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.180
our data into
geospatial buckets,

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:18.580
like S2 cells or geohashes.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:20.350
So what you're seeing
here on the right

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:23.140
is a map of temperature
changes, hyperlocal ones--

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:26.110
and as I've learned in my short
time here in San Francisco,

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.330
the weather changes when
you change streets or turn

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:30.730
corners-- and so this
is really, really cool.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.820
And we can do boxes of
500 feet by 500 feet

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:37.900
and actually aggregate anonymize
our data and show it on a map.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.810
And we can also do things like
create inline functions here

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:42.680
to do haversine distance.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.370
So we've been doing
geospatial for some time.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:48.550
But the GIS functions
accelerate this development

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:51.730
and really, really allow us to
build smart city applications

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:54.490
on top of our already
existing platform.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:55.870
So that map you
saw before, where

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:59.080
we could bin it into
S2 cells or we could

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:03.160
look at using geohashes,
now what we can do

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:05.920
is we can aggregate and
anonymize and pull statistics

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:07.690
across arbitrary shapes.

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.960
So it could be a GeoJSON file,
it could be a shape file,

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:14.830
and we can perform all of those
cool ST_* functions that Chad

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:16.810
said right within BigQuery.

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.420
So now we can do distance
and intersection and contains

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:20.960
and all those cool things.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.520
So that map we had before--

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:25.300
where we were looking at boxes
on the earth-- was really cool,

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:27.770
but we can also now just go
out and get some open data.

00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:29.561
So I took an open data
set of neighborhoods

00:16:29.561 --> 00:16:33.340
here in San Francisco, and
I can use an ST contains

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.110
and just find what
the temperature

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:37.060
is in every single one
of these neighborhoods.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:39.220
And we can do this with
anything that we have

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.522
in our big data environment.

00:16:40.522 --> 00:16:42.730
So now we can look at which
neighborhood has the most

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:44.710
accidents or the most
hazardous driving,

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.330
or we can look for
hotspots of idling

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.070
and make an
environmental impact.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:50.240
Really, really cool
stuff that we can do.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.790
And what makes it
even more powerful

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.640
for us is that every single
smart city out there--

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:56.430
or every aspiring smart city--

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:59.520
have their data in
shape files or GeoJSON,

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:01.140
and they also have
Geotab devices

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:04.647
in every single one
of those cities.

00:17:04.647 --> 00:17:07.230
So I'm going to talk to you guys
about a real world use case--

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.271
and we've been starting
to develop an application

00:17:09.271 --> 00:17:12.810
around this that builds upon
our open platform that involves

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.235
driving pattern and driving
behavior, and looking

00:17:15.235 --> 00:17:16.859
at it in front of a
school in New York.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.150
So I'm sure a lot
of you have driven

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.970
over these things in the past--

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.690
and so let's
imagine for a second

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:28.150
that you're dropping your
children off at school in New

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:30.950
York City and you noticed
that, in your opinion,

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:33.440
there are some people driving
in front of the school very,

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.717
very quickly, and so
you make a call into 311

00:17:35.717 --> 00:17:38.050
and you voice your concern--
you're a concerned citizen.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:39.610
And so what might
happen today is

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.550
someone would go out and install
pneumatic tubes across the road

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.379
and conduct a study, and they're
measuring the volume of traffic

00:17:45.379 --> 00:17:47.170
that comes across those
tubes and the speed

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:48.610
that they're traveling.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.220
And they collect the data for a
few weeks, they bring it back,

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:52.844
they analyze it, and
then they get back

00:17:52.844 --> 00:17:55.559
to you, the concerned
citizen, with the results.

00:17:55.559 --> 00:17:57.100
And what we think
is that now that we

00:17:57.100 --> 00:18:00.490
have these geospatial functions,
we can spend a few minutes

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:03.940
and do a virtual study with a
virtual pneumatic tube using

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:07.180
the new functions in BQ GIS.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:08.650
So before we get
to the demo, I'm

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.108
just going to do a couple of
little under the covers things

00:18:11.108 --> 00:18:14.000
to showcase how we use some
of the geospatial functions.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.720
So the first thing we wanted
to do for this demo was we

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:18.970
don't need to look at
1.2 million vehicles

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:21.190
to figure out what the
traffic looks like in front

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:22.720
of a school in New York.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.130
So how can we filter that data?

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:26.350
In the past, maybe we
would've used the bounding box

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.110
and we would have tried to
use latitude/longitude--

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:31.630
but now we go to the
NYC OpenData portal

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.954
and we can pull down the
data set of school zones.

00:18:33.954 --> 00:18:35.620
And we know, in this
case, we're looking

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.140
at the Neighborhood Charter
School in District 6

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:39.640
of New York City.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.340
So let's filter by District
6 in New York City.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:44.920
So you can see here we would
pull our GPS records in, which

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:47.557
would contain all of that
traffic information, speed,

00:18:47.557 --> 00:18:50.140
cool stuff that we can get that
you can't get from a pneumatic

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.630
strip-- like types of
vehicles that are driving over

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:53.800
the strip, et cetera--

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.232
and we would use an ST contains.

00:18:55.232 --> 00:18:57.190
And what I thought was
actually especially cool

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:00.080
about this was the NYC
OpenData portal had

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.860
the data sitting in a CSV, so
there was no transformation.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.980
I just downloaded,
uploaded, and ran my query.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:06.955
Pretty awesome.

00:19:06.955 --> 00:19:08.252
AUDIENCE: That's cool.

00:19:08.252 --> 00:19:10.210
BOB BRADLEY: Now the
second thing we need to do

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:13.120
is we need to understand which
vehicles are actually crossing

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:16.480
this fake pneumatic tube,
and the way we do that

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.660
is by leveraging the lag and
lead operators in BigQuery.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.570
So you can imagine as you're
driving your vehicle along,

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.952
you're dropping GPS
points on the road.

00:19:24.952 --> 00:19:27.160
And what we're doing is
we're taking those GPS points

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:28.576
that you can see
in red, and we're

00:19:28.576 --> 00:19:31.000
connecting the lag point
with the current point

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.200
to make a line.

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:34.909
So we're making thousands
and thousands of lines

00:19:34.909 --> 00:19:36.450
as they're dropping
their GPS points,

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:38.860
and what's important to
us is which of those lines

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:42.180
intersects with the fake tube
that we've drawn in the ground.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.070
And so you can see
here, our subquery

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:45.265
is looking at traffic--

00:19:45.265 --> 00:19:47.140
and so here's where
we're making those lines.

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:49.780
So we're using an
ST MakeLine function

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:52.560
and we're taking the lag
point and the current point

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.310
that we have sitting
in BigQuery and we're

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:56.260
making thousands,
if not millions,

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.930
of these tiny little lines.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.760
And down below here, we're
using the Intersects function

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:03.760
to look at which of those
intersect with the line

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:05.500
that we drew on
the map so we can

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.958
get a true representative of
the vehicles that are actually

00:20:07.958 --> 00:20:10.390
crossing in front
of this school.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:12.989
So with that, I'll jump
out and do a quick demo.

00:20:12.989 --> 00:20:14.530
So what you can see
here is an add-in

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.140
that we've made that lives
within our open portal.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:19.720
So we have hundreds, if not
thousands, of these add-ins

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:21.896
now that are built
upon our portal.

00:20:21.896 --> 00:20:23.270
We built some of
those ourselves,

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:24.520
and that's what we have here--

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.220
and I've left myself
a pin because there

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:27.320
is no way in front
of all of you I

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:29.930
was going to be able to find the
school that I wanted to go to.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:31.480
So what we have here is the
Neighborhood Charter School

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:32.810
that we were talking about.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.580
And if I zoom in, my complaint
as a citizen in New York

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.960
was that I feel
like 145th Street,

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:41.270
the traffic is just
moving too quickly.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.030
So what I can do is
I can come over here

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:45.710
and I can create this
virtual pneumatic tube,

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:47.970
and I'm going to draw it
right across 145th Street--

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:49.980
whoops.

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:52.920
And once I have the tube drawn,
I can run the traffic study.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.160
And what we're doing is
we're going out to BigQuery--

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:58.490
Chad, you got any advice here?

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:02.886
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:02.886 --> 00:21:04.760
CHAD JENNINGS: That's
not supposed to happen.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:05.050
BOB BRADLEY: That's not.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.217
So what's happening now,
while Chad's logging in, is

00:21:07.217 --> 00:21:09.675
we're going out to BigQuery
and we're looking for all those

00:21:09.675 --> 00:21:12.190
vehicles that are crossing
that line-- thanks, Chad--

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:13.880
and we're pulling
back statistics.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.780
So now, as an employee of
the city or the municipality,

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:19.610
I can get some information that
I can get back to the citizen.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.420
So I can see, and I
know that in New York,

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:23.920
the speed limit in
front of a school

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.219
should be 15 miles an hour.

00:21:25.219 --> 00:21:27.010
So on the surface, it
doesn't look too bad.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:28.426
If I break it down
by hour of day,

00:21:28.426 --> 00:21:30.310
it looks like people
are pretty well-behaved.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.560
But if you look at these
average speed bans over here,

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.750
I can see 16% of the people
are going between 25 and 30,

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:38.960
another 3 and 1/2% are
going over 30 miles an hour.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:41.110
So there are actually some
20% of the people that

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:43.650
are speeding through this area
quite substantially, so there

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:44.750
may be something to that.

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:48.112
I also get a breakdown of the
types of vehicles that we see,

00:21:48.112 --> 00:21:49.570
the average speed,
and the trends--

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:52.354
and I also notice that Saturday
is a particularly bad day.

00:21:52.354 --> 00:21:54.520
People are driving much,
much quicker on a Saturday.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:55.930
Now, of course, kids
aren't in school that day,

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:57.460
which is great,
but still something

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:58.840
to be concerned about.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.450
The other thing we did
is we used the District 6

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:03.700
to go out and pull in
information that we already

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:05.560
calculate at Geotab
for smart cities

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:08.950
around hazardous driving
areas and road impediments.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.750
So we can see here,
the green represents

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:12.340
areas that are
hazardous, so that's

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:16.270
where we see a lot of harsh
braking events or swerving

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.520
or accidents happening
on aggregate across all

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:19.580
of our vehicles.

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:21.730
So while in front of the
school is not too bad,

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:24.190
just down the street at the
corner is pretty hazardous--

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.685
and this could cause
issues that back up traffic

00:22:26.685 --> 00:22:28.810
or maybe that's a path that
a lot of these students

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:29.960
are walking to school.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.380
And in blue, we measure
the road conditions--

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.690
so good news, in front of
the school is pretty good,

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:36.526
over here maybe could
use some attention.

00:22:36.526 --> 00:22:37.900
So you can see
the kind of things

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.940
that we can do using
the GIS functions.

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:43.320
And what's awesome about this
as a developer is that it's just

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:44.156
a select statement.

00:22:44.156 --> 00:22:46.030
There's no other work
that needs to be done--

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:48.574
and all of our data gets
to stay within BigQuery,

00:22:48.574 --> 00:22:49.990
whereas in the
past, we might have

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:52.370
had to pull it out and then
compare it with shape files.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:54.970
Everything right now happens
right inside BigQuery.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:56.034
So that's it for my demo.

00:22:56.034 --> 00:22:57.700
I'm going to pass
things over to Daniel,

00:22:57.700 --> 00:23:00.116
who's going to show you a much,
much cooler demo combining

00:23:00.116 --> 00:23:02.746
BQ GIS and BQ ML Thank you.

00:23:02.746 --> 00:23:06.092
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:11.970
DANIEL LEWIS: Cool.

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:13.740
All right, so I'm
going to talk to you

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:17.860
about combining BQ ML with
the BigQuery GIS functions.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:23.520
So for those of you who missed
the spotlight session earlier--

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.410
which seems like it
was most of you--

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.100
we demoed the tool
here, which I'm going

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:30.360
to get into more details about.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.490
But essentially,
on the left here

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:35.100
is the current hazardous areas
for the city of Chicago--

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.140
and Bob mentioned the
accelerometer data

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:37.950
that we get--

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.630
and we can aggregate
those over time

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:41.940
to see where there are
particular problem areas, where

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:43.830
we're consistently
seeing relative

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:46.634
and scale to the proportion
of vehicles that are driving

00:23:46.634 --> 00:23:49.050
through that area, and the
volume of traffic where there's

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:51.910
problems and patterns of
hazardous driving behavior.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:55.530
And on the right is
actually a prediction

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:57.060
of where these
hazardous areas are

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.149
expected to be based on
predicted weather conditions.

00:24:00.149 --> 00:24:01.523
And it's going to
be really cool.

00:24:01.523 --> 00:24:02.940
I'm going to walk you
through how we actually

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.620
built this demo, and all of
it happened within BigQuery.

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:07.380
As Bob mentioned, we
never have to leave

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:08.460
using the new functions.

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:11.140
So as a data scientist, I love
playing with machine learning

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:14.730
models, obviously, but now that
I get to use these GIS tools as

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:16.740
well combined with
them, we can do

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:18.750
some pretty awesome and
powerful things that

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:21.017
were really difficult before.

00:24:21.017 --> 00:24:22.350
So just stepping back a moment--

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.160
I mean, Bob already
mentioned this briefly,

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:26.701
but I just want to talk again
about how we actually calculate

00:24:26.701 --> 00:24:27.700
these hazardous areas.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.783
So we've got our devices
in all of these vehicles,

00:24:29.783 --> 00:24:32.550
and we're constantly
monitoring the accelerometer.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:34.410
And so the types of
harsh driving behavior

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:35.785
that we're looking
for are things

00:24:35.785 --> 00:24:39.210
like harsh braking, rapid
acceleration, harsh cornering

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:42.361
relative to the speed of the
street where we wouldn't expect

00:24:42.361 --> 00:24:43.860
that kind of thing
to be happening--

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:45.930
and we're also going to be
detecting accidents constantly

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:46.480
as well.

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.250
And we aggregate
these over time,

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:50.730
and when we look at the
accelerometer events

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:53.610
beyond a certain threshold for
multiple vehicles in this area,

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:56.280
we can start to classify
it as hazardous.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:59.250
And we're constantly calculating
these areas every single day,

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:00.900
globally, across
the entire planet

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.659
and storing them
in BigQuery daily.

00:25:02.659 --> 00:25:04.200
And so if you're
the city of Chicago,

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.600
obviously you don't care
about all of this global data,

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:07.500
you're just going
want to filter out

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:10.050
the subset of areas that are
relevant to you in the city.

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:11.970
And using these
cool GIS tools, they

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:13.530
can just upload a
shape file to us,

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:15.630
and then we can run a
simple select statement

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.630
and pull out all of our data
within that red bounding box

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:20.440
area for the city of Chicago--

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:21.990
which is super cool
and a lot easier

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:23.604
than what I had to do before.

00:25:23.604 --> 00:25:25.770
Before, when we were still
doing this kind of stuff,

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.350
I would have had to do things
like cache a table that

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:31.440
had reverse geocoded
S2 cells or geohashes,

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:34.200
look for within certain
bounds, that kind of thing.

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:36.910
But now it's a one liner,
which is fantastic.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:42.130
But what I think is even
cooler than aggregating

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.290
this information
over time is what

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:46.220
we can do in terms of
predicting the future.

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:48.280
So I mean, cities are obviously
really interested in what's

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:50.280
happening right now in
terms of hazardous areas,

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:51.760
and they're going
to want to know

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:54.280
the severity of those things
and how to prioritize roadwork

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:56.200
and maybe improve
signage, add things

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:59.200
like speed bumps and things
like that in the road.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:01.360
But what's more
important is actually

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:04.090
being proactive and
addressing safety concerns

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:05.980
before they actually
start to hurt people,

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:08.110
especially in front of
things like schools.

00:26:08.110 --> 00:26:09.580
And so I thought
a really cool use

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:11.913
case would be use some of the
public data that's already

00:26:11.913 --> 00:26:14.860
available in BigQuery to
build a model around weather

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:17.440
and see how, if it's going to
be a particularly snowy day

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.190
or if it's going to
be a really rainy day,

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.850
we're losing visibility, how
is that going to impact traffic

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:22.710
around the city.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.390
And the city can
maybe say, OK, we're

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:25.960
going to have a particularly
harsh winter with a lot

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:28.430
more traffic volume than we're
used to, what's going to happen

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:29.888
and what areas do
I need to address

00:26:29.888 --> 00:26:31.187
before that becomes a problem.

00:26:31.187 --> 00:26:32.770
So stepping back a
little bit, first I

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:34.187
need to actually
do a sanity check

00:26:34.187 --> 00:26:35.561
to see if that's
something that's

00:26:35.561 --> 00:26:36.670
even going to be possible.

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:39.550
So I took the last two years
of our hazardous driving

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:41.410
area for the city
of Chicago, started

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:43.810
to parse it out by weather
conditions, and looked--

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:47.350
was there any measurable
impact in our data from weather

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:49.780
on the relative severity
of hazardous driving

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:50.930
across the city.

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:53.260
And so I used a variety
of the GIS functions,

00:26:53.260 --> 00:26:56.050
but essentially here, the ST
Snap to Grid functionality

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:58.090
to bin things across the
city and then calculate

00:26:58.090 --> 00:27:00.430
the average severity score
and correlate it with weather

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:01.888
and produce some
of these cool maps

00:27:01.888 --> 00:27:03.350
that you see here on the screen.

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:04.930
And so you're going to notice
they're a little bit jiggled

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:07.750
around there, so after doing
the aggregation with the ST Snap

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.850
to Grid functionality, I
just shifted them around

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:12.004
by the average latitude
and longitude for each

00:27:12.004 --> 00:27:14.170
of those sections where
we're historically observing

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:16.810
hazardous driving
areas so that we

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:18.430
can tell the city
specifically where

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:21.560
the problem is on the road so
they can go and address that.

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:23.150
I mean, after
producing these maps,

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:25.250
my first thought was,
cool, this is awesome.

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:27.140
I can immediately see a big
difference between the winter

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:29.190
and the summer in these
particular days here.

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:31.919
So there's probably something
that I can do with a model

00:27:31.919 --> 00:27:34.460
that I'm going to be building
in BigQuery to start to predict

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:35.900
these things before
they happen and see

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.025
what kind of the relationships
are, mathematically,

00:27:38.025 --> 00:27:40.070
between weather and the
hazardous driving areas.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:42.800
I mean, one of the cool things
that was non-obvious to me that

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:46.340
jumped out from this was on
the left, you see, obviously,

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:49.009
there's a lot more hazardous
driving and hazardous areas

00:27:49.009 --> 00:27:50.300
in the winter than the summer--

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:52.910
but the highways actually are
proportionally less dangerous

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:54.170
than the neighborhoods
of the city, which

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.030
was a little bit
counter-intuitive to me.

00:27:56.030 --> 00:27:59.030
So you're possibly more likely
to get into an accident going

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:00.920
to or from the
highway than actually

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:02.144
driving on the highway--

00:28:02.144 --> 00:28:04.310
just because of the amount
of harsh driving relative

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:06.800
to the volume of traffic
that's going through that area.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.080
So great, this seems
viable-- and historical data

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:10.700
is really interesting,
but how can we

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:12.824
actually use that to start
to make some predictions

00:28:12.824 --> 00:28:13.742
about the future?

00:28:13.742 --> 00:28:15.950
So we're going to dive in
a little bit under the hood

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:19.996
into how we actually made
the spotlight session happen.

00:28:19.996 --> 00:28:22.370
So yeah, it's really easy to
pull in all of these things.

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:24.950
We can aggregate
with some simple ST

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:27.504
functions the hazardous
areas for the city

00:28:27.504 --> 00:28:29.420
over the past few weeks
or the past few months

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.294
or whatever particular
historical period they

00:28:31.294 --> 00:28:34.190
might be interested in, maybe
filter that by severity score

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.106
and give them an idea
of where they might want

00:28:36.106 --> 00:28:37.592
to prioritize their roadwork.

00:28:37.592 --> 00:28:39.050
And for a city,
this is super cool,

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:41.014
but I mean, if I'm
a city planner,

00:28:41.014 --> 00:28:43.680
I want to address things, again,
proactively before they happen.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:44.990
So what about tomorrow?

00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:46.490
What about tomorrow
if it's snowing?

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:47.948
What about tomorrow
if it's snowing

00:28:47.948 --> 00:28:49.170
and there's low visibility?

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:51.050
There's all sorts of conditions
that could happen tomorrow,

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:52.520
and maybe you could
go back and find

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.103
an exact historical
correlate for what

00:28:54.103 --> 00:28:56.750
had happened with that, but
that doesn't take into account

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:59.427
your projected traffic volume,
your speeds on these streets

00:28:59.427 --> 00:29:01.760
change over time, the weather
patterns change over time.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.180
A lot of things are changing.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:04.550
And so if you don't
understand the relationships

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:06.716
between those, I can't just
look at historical data.

00:29:06.716 --> 00:29:08.780
I need to be able to
predict the future.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:11.030
And so that's what we're
going to do using just BQ ML,

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.920
and we never have
to leave BigQuery

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:16.000
to do any of this, which
is the coolest part.

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:20.020
And so we can join
the data that we have,

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.712
our historical driving data,
with all of the public data

00:29:22.712 --> 00:29:24.670
I mentioned that BigQuery
already has available

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:25.720
for daily weather.

00:29:25.720 --> 00:29:27.942
They have this going back
decades for thousands

00:29:27.942 --> 00:29:29.650
of weather stations
across North America,

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:32.050
and there's several of them that
happen to be around Chicago.

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:33.790
So I can interpolate
between all of those

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:36.070
and get, roughly,
for that day things

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:39.100
like the average temperature
broken down by that grid

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:41.325
that we did with ST Snap
to Grid functionality.

00:29:41.325 --> 00:29:43.450
I can get the max temperature,
the min temperature,

00:29:43.450 --> 00:29:46.150
the visibility for that day, the
total amount of precipitation,

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:47.500
whether it was
foggy, whether there

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:49.208
was funnel cloud
formation for tornadoes,

00:29:49.208 --> 00:29:50.680
all of this cool things.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:54.460
And then I can use
all these new BQ ML

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:56.620
tools which have been
announced several times

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:58.592
today to start to
train on this data.

00:29:58.592 --> 00:30:00.800
And so in just a few lines
of code that you see here,

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:02.590
I'm able to train a model
that's going to be doing

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:03.920
all of this prediction for me.

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:06.380
So a few lines of code, really
simple, really powerful.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:09.070
So in reality, it
actually created over

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:10.750
300 features for
this model, which

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:13.060
are various, transformed
versions of weather

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:15.790
inputs, combinations-- things
like temperature scaled

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:18.340
by visibility and
functional transformations

00:30:18.340 --> 00:30:19.514
and things like that.

00:30:19.514 --> 00:30:21.430
Unfortunately, that
wouldn't fit on the slide,

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:23.013
and Chad wouldn't
let me do it anyway,

00:30:23.013 --> 00:30:25.729
so this is the simplified
version of that.

00:30:25.729 --> 00:30:27.520
So if you just sort of
use your imagination

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:30.520
and the temperature,
visibility, snow, rain, and fog

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:34.670
here are actually reflective
of much more complex features.

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:37.180
But there's a whole lot of
things going on here that you

00:30:37.180 --> 00:30:39.554
see on the slide, and there's
actually even more going on

00:30:39.554 --> 00:30:41.060
behind the scenes,
which I'm also

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:42.643
going to talk about,
and which I think

00:30:42.643 --> 00:30:46.194
is the really key part of
what makes BQ ML so powerful--

00:30:46.194 --> 00:30:48.610
and what was going to really
make it powerful for analysts

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.280
as well who maybe don't have as
much data science background.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:52.360
And so on the first
line, we're just

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.100
telling BigQuery to
create a new model--

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:54.999
simple enough.

00:30:54.999 --> 00:30:57.040
And then we can provide
some options for BigQuery

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:58.915
and how it's going to
actually fit this model

00:30:58.915 --> 00:31:01.762
and what kind of hyperparameters
you want us to use to do that.

00:31:01.762 --> 00:31:03.220
But the only actual
required things

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.360
here are the model type and the
labels that I want to predict.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.110
We can specify a whole
lot of other things

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:11.230
too, though, such as the minimum
relative progress, the learning

00:31:11.230 --> 00:31:14.200
rate that I want it to use, if
I want it to do regularization,

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:16.810
what type of regularization,
the maximum number of training

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:19.360
iterations, the learning rate
strategy-- all of these things

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:21.551
I can tell BigQuery to
do, but I don't have to

00:31:21.551 --> 00:31:23.800
because BigQuery is going
to handle all of that for me

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:25.758
and do this kind of
hyperparameter optimization

00:31:25.758 --> 00:31:26.364
for me.

00:31:26.364 --> 00:31:28.030
BigQuery's automatically
going to create

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:29.350
a train/test split of my data.

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:31.150
I can manually specify
that if I want to,

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:32.530
but again, I don't have to.

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:34.480
It's going to do the
train/test split for me.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:36.160
It's going to set an
initial learning rate,

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:37.870
and it's going adjust it
during gradient descent

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:38.536
algorithmically.

00:31:38.536 --> 00:31:40.203
I don't have to worry
about that either.

00:31:40.203 --> 00:31:41.952
One of the coolest
things, especially when

00:31:41.952 --> 00:31:44.320
you have over 300 features,
is that it's going to center

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:46.944
and normalize all of my features
to zero mean and unit standard

00:31:46.944 --> 00:31:49.900
deviation without me having
to do any of that-- saves me

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:51.520
a heck of a lot of typing.

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:53.680
It's also going to
automatically one hot encode all

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:54.970
of my categorical variables.

00:31:54.970 --> 00:31:56.540
I don't have to do that either.

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:58.060
And I think there's going to be
some additional functionality

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:00.059
soon that you can
automatically bucketize things

00:32:00.059 --> 00:32:01.226
as well, which is fantastic.

00:32:01.226 --> 00:32:03.767
And because it's automatically
creating this train/test split

00:32:03.767 --> 00:32:06.520
for me, it's actually keeping
track of the loss by iteration,

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:08.620
so I can immediately go, as soon
as this model has been trained,

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:10.330
and quickly check for
overfitting, which again,

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.070
is fantastic-- and
I don't have to code

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:13.180
any of that behind the scenes.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:15.013
I can do this with just
a few lines of code,

00:32:15.013 --> 00:32:16.234
which is fantastic.

00:32:16.234 --> 00:32:17.650
And just to
reiterate, all of this

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:18.870
is happening inside BigQuery.

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:20.786
I still haven't even
left the same browser tab

00:32:20.786 --> 00:32:22.815
that I opened to begin
with, which is fantastic.

00:32:22.815 --> 00:32:24.190
So this particular
model that I'm

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.648
going to actually show you the
demo for, I trained on about

00:32:26.648 --> 00:32:29.480
600,000 training examples--

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:31.240
and the query for
training the model only

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:32.516
took about 15 minutes.

00:32:32.516 --> 00:32:33.890
And if you remember,
I mentioned,

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:35.500
I've got over 300
features, so that's

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:37.480
over 180 million
data points used

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:40.390
for training that BigQuery's
able to parse through and fit

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:42.730
a model to in under 15
minutes, which is fantastic,

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.690
using nothing but SQL code.

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:48.540
So OK, you've-- yup, I'm going
to take a breath for a moment.

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:49.950
There's a lot of things
happening in that slide,

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:50.490
but OK.

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:51.180
You're the analyst.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:53.020
You've set up this model, you've
gone, you've got your coffee,

00:32:53.020 --> 00:32:54.937
you come and sit down
and wow, OK, the model's

00:32:54.937 --> 00:32:55.603
already trained.

00:32:55.603 --> 00:32:56.130
Fantastic.

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:58.590
Let's start making
predictions-- but not quite.

00:32:58.590 --> 00:32:59.760
Is it actually any good?

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.380
Luckily, this
functionality is already

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:03.060
built into BigQuery for us.

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:04.752
So again, super simple.

00:33:04.752 --> 00:33:06.710
Just a few lines of code
that you can see here,

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:08.790
in the rather self-explanatory
on the slide--

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:10.650
but I still haven't
left the same tab.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.870
I can do all of my
evaluation off of tables

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:16.190
that BigQuery's already created
when I trained this model.

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.690
We've already got what we need
for a basic overfitting check

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:20.481
because it did that
train/test split for me

00:33:20.481 --> 00:33:22.080
and created that loss table.

00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:24.342
But we can also easily use
this evaluate statement

00:33:24.342 --> 00:33:25.800
to pull out some
summary statistics

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:27.508
like the mean squared
error of the model,

00:33:27.508 --> 00:33:29.040
the r-squared scored,
the percentage

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.140
of explained variance.

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:31.625
And I can see if
we're on the mark

00:33:31.625 --> 00:33:33.750
or if we need to maybe go
back to our data cleaning

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:36.570
step, our feature generation
or selection stage,

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:38.310
and train again.

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:40.880
Luckily for us, I've already
done all this dirty work

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:42.630
and we can just skip
right ahead to making

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:45.005
some cool predictions about
where the dangerous areas are

00:33:45.005 --> 00:33:46.960
going to be.

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:48.452
So after the last
couple of slides,

00:33:48.452 --> 00:33:50.160
this is probably going
to sound familiar,

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:51.690
but we can do all
of our predictions

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:53.481
with just a few lines
of code-- and I still

00:33:53.481 --> 00:33:55.260
haven't left the same
open browser tab.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:57.040
It's really, really cool.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:34:00.000
So the syntax here is almost
identical to the Ml.Evaluate

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:01.710
statement that we
were running earlier.

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:03.030
We basically just
pass in the model

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:04.613
that we've already
created and trained

00:34:04.613 --> 00:34:06.915
and hopefully, as a diligent
data scientist or analyst,

00:34:06.915 --> 00:34:09.540
you've already gone through and
done the appropriate evaluation

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.610
to make sure that
your model's actually

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:13.685
predicting something
and explaining

00:34:13.685 --> 00:34:15.060
an appropriate
amount of variance

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:17.020
that you can use
it for something.

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:18.219
And so I've done that.

00:34:18.219 --> 00:34:20.219
And so what is this
actually going to look like,

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:22.469
and how could a city use
this to actually do something

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:23.917
actionable with this data?

00:34:23.917 --> 00:34:25.500
And so I'm going to
jump into the demo

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:27.459
and show you what that
app actually looks like.

00:34:27.459 --> 00:34:29.500
So this was from the
screenshot that I showed you

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:30.989
at the beginning
of this section.

00:34:30.989 --> 00:34:32.550
On the left here,
just to reiterate,

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.949
was the current hazardous
driving areas in Chicago--

00:34:34.949 --> 00:34:36.420
this is real data, by the way.

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:37.053
All of this.

00:34:37.053 --> 00:34:38.969
So this is the current
hazardous driving areas

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:41.219
in the city of Chicago that
we measured and aggregated

00:34:41.219 --> 00:34:42.479
over the last couple of weeks.

00:34:42.479 --> 00:34:44.520
So you can see that
particular areas of the city,

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:46.560
specifically in the
south of Chicago,

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:48.929
are kind of dangerous to be
driving around right now.

00:34:48.929 --> 00:34:51.272
And a city might want to
zoom in on these areas

00:34:51.272 --> 00:34:53.730
and figure out exactly where
these problems are and address

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:55.900
them, but as I'm
trying to reiterate,

00:34:55.900 --> 00:34:57.930
it's better to be
proactive than reactive

00:34:57.930 --> 00:35:00.240
in addressing these
kinds of concerns.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:02.850
And so let's say that
it's coming up to the fall

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:05.190
and it's going to be a
particularly cold, snowy day,

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:08.287
and let's drop the
temperature down a little bit.

00:35:08.287 --> 00:35:10.120
So let's say it's going
to be, I don't know,

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:12.310
maybe 31 degrees on a
particular day-- and there's

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:13.893
a lot of snow, so
maybe I'll drop down

00:35:13.893 --> 00:35:15.090
the visibility a little bit.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:16.840
We'll say that it's
snowing, and let's say

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:18.370
it's the holiday season
and people are really

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:19.760
excited to get out
and do their shopping,

00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:21.740
and more people are on
the road than usual.

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:24.790
I'd say maybe traffic volume is
20% higher than it usually is.

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:26.290
So let's run the
predictive analysis

00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:27.550
and see what the
model thinks is going

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:29.740
to be the dangerous areas
in the city of Chicago

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:32.650
for that particularly snowy,
cold day with a lot of traffic.

00:35:35.931 --> 00:35:36.430
Boom.

00:35:36.430 --> 00:35:37.730
So the map looks
really different.

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.688
So some of the areas that
were hazardous before

00:35:39.688 --> 00:35:41.080
are still hazardous, so great.

00:35:41.080 --> 00:35:42.340
I mean, the city
definitely knows now

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:43.540
to prioritize those areas.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:46.570
But what's really cool is that
areas in the top left here

00:35:46.570 --> 00:35:48.880
and in the north of this
city which weren't dangerous

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:50.380
before are actually
predicted to get

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:51.640
quite hazardous on this day.

00:35:51.640 --> 00:35:54.070
So if a city's just
basing their actions

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:55.987
for fixing road conditions
on historical data,

00:35:55.987 --> 00:35:57.944
they're going to miss a
lot of these areas that

00:35:57.944 --> 00:36:00.680
can become quite dangerous with
different weather conditions.

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:02.770
And so if we actually zoom
in on a particular area

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.717
here that I found before when
looking through the data,

00:36:05.717 --> 00:36:07.300
one of these predicted
dangerous areas

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:08.980
is right next to a school.

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:10.780
So you're looking
at historical data,

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:12.488
the city's not going
to know that there's

00:36:12.488 --> 00:36:14.630
this dangerous problem
right in front of a school.

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.240
And so with this cool
tool, we can actually

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:18.198
use our little Google
person and jump down here

00:36:18.198 --> 00:36:19.250
and see what's happening.

00:36:19.250 --> 00:36:20.791
So if we drop down
a little bit ahead

00:36:20.791 --> 00:36:24.547
of where this hazardous area
is happening and look around,

00:36:24.547 --> 00:36:26.380
we can see the school
there, which is cool--

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:27.870
and actually, if you
notice up in the distance,

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:30.161
there is a student cross-walk
up there, which is great.

00:36:30.161 --> 00:36:32.200
That's good signage
for safe areas.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:34.060
But if we look around
here, we actually

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:36.070
see that the bike racks
are way down here,

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:37.060
next to this alleyway.

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:39.560
So students are probably coming
in and out of this alleyway,

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:41.530
and if we step over
here a little bit,

00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:43.510
we notice there's no
stop signs and no signage

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:44.770
on this alleyway either.

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:46.464
There's no speed
bumps or nothing.

00:36:46.464 --> 00:36:48.130
So the hazardous area
is happening right

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:51.117
before this alleyway here.

00:36:51.117 --> 00:36:53.200
And the nearest signage
is several hundred feet up

00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:54.500
the road near this
crossing sign,

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:55.510
but it looks like
most of the students

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:57.010
are actually crossing
here-- so the city might

00:36:57.010 --> 00:36:59.380
want to come out and maybe think
about putting some speed humps

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:01.270
here or some additional
signage, or maybe

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:02.380
some mirrors around
these corners

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:04.963
and addressing this problem that
wouldn't otherwise be obvious

00:37:04.963 --> 00:37:07.464
based on their current
historical data.

00:37:07.464 --> 00:37:09.130
So hopefully you found
that interesting.

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:11.213
I found it super cool to
play with these new tools

00:37:11.213 --> 00:37:14.060
and build these models out-- and
never having to leave BigQuery,

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:17.050
and we're able to train
this model on this data.

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:18.634
The actual model
trains in 15 minutes,

00:37:18.634 --> 00:37:20.341
but even going through
all of the feature

00:37:20.341 --> 00:37:22.240
generation and the
ability to quickly iterate

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:24.869
through training and evaluating
within the BigQuery environment

00:37:24.869 --> 00:37:26.410
enabled me to put
this model together

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:27.951
in a matter of weeks,
which is really

00:37:27.951 --> 00:37:29.890
quick turnaround for
something this powerful.

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:32.380
So with that, I'm going to
turn it over back to Chad.

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:33.570
Thank you.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:37.330
[APPLAUSE]

