00:37:43.160 --> 00:37:45.400
want to touch on the timing.

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:48.400
So we reached out-- we've been
working together for a while,

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:51.670
but we reached out to you
to talk about like, hey,

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:54.910
I think there's good material
for a demo for next year--

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:57.230
and that was really
only two months ago.

00:37:57.230 --> 00:38:00.040
And so getting spun up
on the GIS features,

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:02.440
getting spun up on BQ
ML, crafting the thing,

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:04.900
and then producing
that awesome demo

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:07.420
that's not just a demo
that's going to be fun for--

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:10.430
the city planners in Chicago
are going to love that.

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:13.240
Anyway-- so I just wanted
to give it up for Geotab,

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:15.190
both the speakers here
and the folks back

00:38:15.190 --> 00:38:16.810
at the home office
who worked on that,

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:18.440
you guys did a fantastic job.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:20.950
Thank you.

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:21.760
Alrighty.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:23.230
So this is the wrap up, right?

00:38:23.230 --> 00:38:27.770
This is the third act of
our three act play, here--

00:38:27.770 --> 00:38:30.622
and I just wanted to
review a little bit.

00:38:30.622 --> 00:38:32.330
We're not going to go
through everything,

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:34.870
but we talked about
what the launches were,

00:38:34.870 --> 00:38:38.020
we talked about how Geotab
is able to craft that,

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.630
not just into a cool
demo, but into a product

00:38:40.630 --> 00:38:42.270
that they can then--

00:38:42.270 --> 00:38:44.272
like these are new
product lines that Geotab

00:38:44.272 --> 00:38:45.980
and other companies
that want to leverage

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:49.610
this can then generate for
smart city applications.

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:53.210
Or, what's also getting
me excited about this--

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:53.950
right?

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:55.570
The third act isn't
the end, right?

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:56.770
There's an encore, right?

00:38:56.770 --> 00:39:00.070
There are ring studies
for retail, ride-sharing

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:06.850
applications, IOT, media
studying DMAs and MSAs.

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:09.350
Using these new features
inside of BigQuery

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:12.730
actually opens BigQuery
up to enormous new volumes

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:15.460
of work, which is what
we're all about, right?

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:18.550
We want to make our tool useful
to as many people as possible

00:39:18.550 --> 00:39:22.130
in as many different
domains as possible.

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:25.160
So that actually brings me
to a final analogy here.

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:28.010
There is quite a lot
of geospatial data

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:30.440
that's already resident
in BigQuery, right?

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.100
Bob was talking that--

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:36.260
Geotab has been doing GIS work
in BigQuery for quite a while,

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:39.080
but there are lots of
columns in BigQuery tables

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:41.930
that have latitude or
longitude as the column header.

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:44.510
So the data, in some
respects, is already there.

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:46.467
Certainly the data is
in other data stores

00:39:46.467 --> 00:39:48.050
that folks haven't
brought to BigQuery

00:39:48.050 --> 00:39:50.810
because we didn't support
latitudes and longitudes.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:52.730
And this is the analogy
with Street View.

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:56.360
So was it back about
2006, 2007, Google

00:39:56.360 --> 00:39:59.180
went through the
world's streets and took

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:03.410
pictures of facades,
street pictures like this,

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:07.070
and just logged where
they were in the world.

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:09.710
We made the Street
View home database.

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:11.450
What we weren't able
to do at the time

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:13.910
that we collected that
data was, in detail,

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:16.580
look into those pictures
and extract information--

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:19.670
like where are the traffic
signs, what are the street

00:40:19.670 --> 00:40:22.160
numbers, what are the
retail establishments that

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:23.600
are at that location.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:25.490
We couldn't do that.

00:40:25.490 --> 00:40:27.860
Since then, we've
developed the ML technology

00:40:27.860 --> 00:40:30.530
to look into these images and
pull that information out--

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:35.720
and we've even productized
those as Cloud ML APIs.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:38.000
And that's generated
an enormous opportunity

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:42.639
for us and for our
customers in that we can now

00:40:42.639 --> 00:40:44.555
look into these data
sets and provide metadata

00:40:44.555 --> 00:40:47.510
on these are the stores,
these are the establishments

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:49.250
that are at this location.

00:40:49.250 --> 00:40:52.970
And so we've actually been able
to generate a lot more value

00:40:52.970 --> 00:40:56.726
off of this trove of images
that we collected a decade ago.

00:40:56.726 --> 00:40:58.100
And that's the
state that I think

00:40:58.100 --> 00:41:02.150
we're in right now in terms of
geospatial data in BigQuery.

00:41:02.150 --> 00:41:04.010
People have been
collecting geospatial data,

00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:05.884
people even have been
storing it in BigQuery,

00:41:05.884 --> 00:41:08.660
but they've lacked the tools
to really go in and dig out

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:11.150
cool stuff like what
Bob and Daniel showed.

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:14.330
So I'm very, very, very
excited about this,

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:16.970
and I think we're at an
inflection point for this type

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:17.960
of work in BigQuery.

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:21.310
